Current Debian kernel release forked from: 6.12.111-1.

This is a change to the amdgpu driver to add some Barco adapter support.
The changes have been integrated into the linux kernel as of June 2026,
but it is unclear when this will make it into an official Debian 13
release.

The changes are needed to recognize some Barco adapters that use the
AMD GPU chipset but nominally are only released or supported for Windows.

The result of having to do this means we cannot support Secure Boot enabled
on CDPW workstations (we cannot build signed kernels). Here we build
a kernel and install that in place of the distribution kernel/modules.

This URL in the Debian kernel handbook that describes how to build this is:

https://kernel-team.pages.debian.net/kernel-handbook/ch-common-tasks.html#s-common-official-vcs

However, results are bit tricky and not quite is reported in the handbook.
The local changes here are to add a patch for the amdgpu driver.

This is a bit hard to automate, but start with the section 4.6 described above
while in this directory:

 sudo apt-get install build-essential rsync git devscripts
 sudo apt-get build-dep linux
 cd debian_kernel_build
 debvian/rules orig
 debian/rules debian/control  # Ignore what looks like an error exit
 dpkg-buildpackage -B -nc -uc

Expect the last step to fail, but not before producing the .deb
file neeeed:

  linux-image-6.12-amd64-unsigned_6.12.XXX-2030~exp1_amd64.deb

where XXX right now is 111 (later it might be something else).


